  "literature": {
    "paper": "Practical long-term investor scenario (own design)",
    "comparison_level": "PRACTICAL SCENARIO, not a literature replication. Asks the investor-relevant question: over a long, multi-regime horizon (2011-2024), does the transformer pipeline (model mu + shared/sample Sigma) pay off against the plain historical pipeline (sample-mean mu + sample Sigma) and against a passive S&P 500 buy-and-hold? Diversified large-cap universe, monthly rebalancing with annual retraining, K=20 with a 10% per-name cap. Universe is filtered to full-history survivors (survivorship bias inflates absolute returns for ALL strategies equally; the transformer-vs-historical comparison is relative and largely unaffected, but the index gap should be read as a lower bound).",
    "reference_values": {
      "note": "No external anchor; headline metrics are long-term terminal wealth, CAGR, annualized Sharpe and maximum drawdown, plus the equity curve vs SPY."
    }
